
  Here's the design, first section.

  [1mSection 1 — The validation module[0m

  [1mNew file: [38;5;153mvalidation.js[0m at the repo root, next to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (not [38;5;153msrc/[39m — that tree is CommonJS Node code and this is
  browser ES-module code; mixing them is what makes the current repo confusing).

  It exports two things:

  [1mRule builders.[0m Each returns a predicate [38;5;153m(value, data) => string | null[39m — [38;5;153mnull[39m means valid, a string is the error
  message.
  - [38;5;153mrequired(message?)[39m
  - [38;5;153mminLength(n, message?)[39m
  - [38;5;153memail(message?)[39m
  - [38;5;153mmatches(otherField, message?)[39m — for the signup form's password confirmation

  Each takes an optional message so a form can override the default wording without a new rule.

  [1mThe validator.[0m
  [33mvalidate[39m(rules, data) [32m// -> { valid: boolean, errors: { [field]: string } }[39m
  It walks each field's rule array in order and stops at that field's first failure, so a field yields one message
  rather than a pile. [38;5;153merrors[39m is [38;5;153m{}[39m when valid. Fields in [38;5;153mdata[39m with no rules are ignored; fields in [38;5;153mrules[39m missing from
  [38;5;153mdata[39m are validated as [38;5;153mundefined[39m (so [38;5;153mrequired()[39m catches them).

  Rules receive the whole [38;5;153mdata[39m object as a second argument — that's what makes [38;5;153mmatches[39m possible without a separate
  cross-field mechanism.

  [1mWhy per-field errors:[0m the current [38;5;153m{ valid, error }[39m returns one string for the whole form. A signup form needs to say
  [3mwhich[0m field is wrong. This is a breaking change to [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m's contract, which is fine since it has exactly one
  caller today.

  Does that look right so far?

